    "description": "Specialty metals contribute particular properties to alloys and other industrial materials. Molybdenum provides a documented Iranian production example, although mine concentrate statistics do not establish domestic production of every downstream specialty product.",
    "tags": ["Specialty Metals", "Molybdenum", "Chromium", "Metallurgy", "Industrial Materials"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T11:44:01Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T11:44:01Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Specialty Metals in Iran: Molybdenum and the Evidence Boundary",
      "description": "Explore molybdenum’s industrial role and Iranian production evidence, with clear distinctions between concentrate, contained metal and finished products."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A category defined by function",
        "content": "For this guide, specialty metals are considered through the particular properties they bring to industrial materials. Molybdenum is an instructive example: its most common use is as an alloying agent in stainless steels, alloy steels and superalloys, improving hardness, strength and corrosion resistance. Its industrial significance therefore cannot be judged by tonnage alone. [s2]\n\nMolybdenite is the principal mineral source of molybdenum. Recognizing the distinction between a mineral feedstock and an alloying material helps explain why evidence of mining does not by itself demonstrate a complete domestic manufacturing chain. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "iran-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the Iranian evidence establishes",
        "content": "USGS reports Iranian molybdenum mine output in both concentrate weight and contained molybdenum. The series below uses contained metal, allowing comparison without treating changing concentrate composition as an equivalent change in metal output. It documents an upstream activity; it does not measure production of purified metal, alloy additions or finished components. [s1]\n\nChromium offers a related materials example. It contributes corrosion and oxidation resistance and is used in stainless steels and superalloys. However, evidence concerning chromium-bearing materials must identify whether it concerns ore, ferrochromium or chromium metal. Those forms have different processing requirements and industrial roles. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "research-boundaries",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From mineral availability to demonstrated capability",
        "content": "The central editorial question is how far a documented supply chain extends. A useful industry account would connect extraction with recovery, refining, product specification and verified industrial use. This draft establishes a molybdenum production series and explains relevant material functions, but it does not establish that entire chain in Iran.\n\nClaims about titanium, rare-earth metals, high-purity products or advanced specialty components require their own inspected evidence. They are excluded here pending research rather than inferred from geological occurrences or broad industrial announcements. This page consequently remains an incomplete category account.",
        "source_ids": []
      },
      {
        "id": "molybdenum-chart",
        "type": "chart",
